.SH LOG OUTPUT
.TP
.B --log-format pretty|pretty-verbose|jsonl
Select compact colored summaries, expanded event metadata, or one JSON event
per line. All audit display modes use redacted copies; environment mappings,
credential fields and recognized sensitive content are omitted from logs.
Native return values and native permission decisions are unchanged.
.TP
.B --no-color
Disable ANSI log colors. NO_COLOR and redirected stderr also disable colors.
Interactive PTY byte passthrough on a real terminal is independent of this
setting; cursor control and split UTF-8 sequences are preserved.
.TP
.B --verbose
Alias for --log-format pretty-verbose.
.TP
.B --raw-events
Alias for --log-format jsonl. --raw remains supported.
.TP
.B --ui-config FILE
Load local TOML display settings. Default: ~/.config/ssmcp/config.toml.
.TP
.B --theme dark|codex-dark|nord|solarized|light|minimal
Select the terminal palette. Default: dark. Individual semantic colors may be
overridden in the local [ui.colors] table.
.TP
.B --timestamps
Include UTC timestamps on event headings.
.TP
.B --max-inline-output N
Show at most N lines per buffered tool output block. Default: 8.
User, agent and answer messages retain their conversation text.

.SH CONVERSATION INTERFACE
The default chat layout shows command activity above a multiline requirement
input, with a collapsible status sidebar. F3 toggles the sidebar; F4 changes
theme, F5 reloads display configuration, and F8 saves the current appearance.
Semantic colors distinguish calls, success, warnings and errors while retaining
text labels. Run ssmcp ui preview to inspect the renderer with fixed samples.
Preview submissions do not contact an agent or restart a real session.
.SS "Requirements and recipients"
Enter queues a local user requirement and releases the editor for the next
draft. Sending waits for server acknowledgement; acknowledgement does not clear
a newer draft. Tab switches input and content focus. Use /queue and the arrow
keys to select requests; Enter takes a pending request back for editing after
server acknowledgement, Delete cancels a retractable request, and Right/Left
opens/closes details.
.PP
Left at the start of an empty or existing draft opens the agent chooser.
Up/Down selects a registered recipient; Enter/Right chooses it and returns to
the conversation. Esc/Left returns with the previous recipient, draft and
cursor preserved. The local /agents command is no longer used.
.PP
Requirements remain queued until an eligible code_mode response carries them,
separately from native execution output. Delivered means attached to a response,
not proof that a model has read or executed it. Exact recipients bind their
collaboration identity for that run. Client and server accept only protocol 7;
a different version is rejected without legacy downgrade. Local attach remains observational; use the foreground client or the
ssmcp.ui.queue primitive to manage requirements.
.PP
Requirements from both the foreground editor and background ssmcp.ui.queue
are written to local conversation history after server acknowledgement.
A requested:true response is not that acknowledgement or proof of delivery.
A history-write failure is reported without reversing an acknowledged request.
.SS "File, skill and image references"
Use @path or @"path with spaces" for a readable regular file in the workspace,
and $skill-name for an effective project or global skill. References are checked
before transmission. Invalid, unreadable, escaping or oversized references
report an error and preserve the source draft. Text files receive a preview of
at most 1 KiB; binary and image references include type, size, path and an actual
native reading method. A path reference does not upload an immutable file copy.
.PP
A requirement supports at most 8 references, files up to 64 MiB, and skill
instructions up to 256 KiB. The draft and expanded requirement must each fit
within 16 KiB. A retry reuses the successfully expanded text, and taking a
request back for editing restores the source notation.
.PP
ssmcp.fs.read_image accepts a workspace-relative image path and returns an MCP
image block with small metadata. PNG, JPEG, GIF and WebP are supported up to
400 KiB (409600 bytes), without silent resizing. After an actual native image
read, the foreground renders a local thumbnail using terminal color cells or
a grayscale fallback. Merely typing @image does not read or decode it.
Local logs do not print the base64 payload. Preview decoding is limited to
4096 pixels per dimension and 64 MiB of decoded pixels.
.PP
PNG and JPEG remain compatible with older relays. Passing GIF or WebP through
image() also requires the relay's updated MIME validator; reloading only the
client does not update that validator. No wire-protocol upgrade is required.
.SS "Clipboard and file import"
Ctrl-V or /paste reads the local operating-system clipboard asynchronously.
Small text enters the active draft or answer; text over 8 KiB and images become
private files referenced by ordinary @ notation. Terminal-handled paste remains
available; use /paste if the terminal intercepts Ctrl-V.
.PP
Use /attach PATH for one regular workspace file; quote paths containing spaces.
Ordinary files retain their path reference. Images are validated and, when
needed, resized or re-encoded into a private .ssmcp/inputs/image-* copy.
Input images are bounded to 16 MiB, 33,554,432 pixels and 16384 pixels per side.
Prepared files fit within 400 KiB of raw image bytes and 4096 pixels per side.
PNG alpha is preserved. Processing reports changed dimensions or encoding;
GIF/WebP converted to PNG/JPEG retain only the first frame and report that limit.
The source image is not overwritten.
.PP
These actions prepare an editable draft without sending it, changing its
recipient or invoking a model. A changed draft, answer or recipient rejects
a late insertion; an already saved file is identified for manual recovery.
Only one local input job runs at a time. Errors preserve new user input.
Private image, long-text and handoff storage currently requires Unix.
Plain clipboard text is never implicitly treated as a file path.
.PP
/copy copies the latest displayed Agent message as original Markdown text,
up to the 16 KiB latest-message cache, using the operating-system clipboard.
An oversized latest reply clears the cache instead of copying an older reply.
A helper failure reports
an error instead of claiming successful copying. Native clipboard helpers
are used only on explicit user action and are not installed automatically.
.SS "Long paste"
On Unix, a bracketed paste exceeding 8 KiB is saved as a private
\&.ssmcp/inputs/paste-<random-id>.txt file and inserted as a quoted @file reference.
Add the request after the reference. A paste may contain at most 4 MiB of UTF-8
text. Capacity and storage failures are reported without silently truncating
the paste. Directories and files are opened without following symbolic links;
the inputs directory is private and new files use mode 0600.
.PP
Automatic paste storage is unavailable on Windows; save a workspace file and
reference it manually. Successful input files are not deleted automatically:
remove them only after later reads are no longer needed. These files are
separate from the request queue and saved conversation history.
.SS "Local agent messages and questions"
Use ssmcp.ui.message for Markdown progress and results addressed to the local
user. Use ssmcp.ui.ask for a nonblocking question: it returns an ID immediately,
so other work may continue. Read the answer later with ssmcp.ui.questions.
This is a local communication convention; it does not intercept messages from
external chat applications.
.PP
Shift-Up opens pending questions. Enter a choice number or free text and press
Enter to save. The answer editor is separate from the normal requirement draft.
Esc returns to the question list; Delete skips a question there; Esc returns
to the conversation. Questions are bounded to 32 pending, 64 retained and
256 KiB in total. Connection reconnects within the same process preserve them;
process restart or /reload does not restore the pending-question queue.
The foreground user supplies answers; background callers may ask and read.
.SS "Events, help and copying"
Buffered tool output uses an 8-line summary by default, with an omission marker
and retained head/tail text. Conversation messages keep their body text.
In the unfiltered chat activity view, each published display block is one
event. The event view retains at most 128 events and 2 MiB of display text,
separately from the audit cache. Long tool events start folded; user, agent,
answer and image events start open.
.PP
With content focused, Up/Down selects an event, Enter toggles it, Right opens
it and Left folds it. Clicking an event also selects and toggles it.
Expansion only reveals retained display text: it does not recover output
already omitted by the summary and never reruns a tool. Read complete output
using the corresponding task cursor or output archive.
.PP
Mouse wheel and PageUp/PageDown scroll content directly. /filter TEXT searches
retained logs while keeping matching command output and completion. Use --all,
--commands or --errors with /filter to select a category. Filtering and other
log layouts retain the existing screen-history view.
.PP
F7 freezes the display and releases mouse capture for native terminal selection.
Use the terminal's copy shortcut, commonly Ctrl-Shift-C; F7 or Esc returns.
Terminals supporting Shift-drag can use that selection gesture directly.
F1 or /help opens full, scrollable help: use arrows, wheel, PageUp/PageDown or
Home/End. F2 cycles activity, audit, agents, queue, questions and history.
.PP
/audit or Ctrl-O opens source audit; Up/Down browses runs, Enter/Right opens
syntax-highlighted source, and Left returns to the list. Source audit uses the
same current protocol 7; older protocol connections are no longer accepted.
.SS "Context handoff"
Use /handoff [note] to prepare a bounded Markdown snapshot of the current
conversation, pending requests and questions. It is saved privately as
\&.ssmcp/inputs/handoff-*.md and inserted as an editable @file draft.
The current recipient is retained; no requirement is automatically sent,
model invoked or external session switched. The document is limited to
2 MiB and the optional note to 16 KiB; omitted content is identified.
It does not include the unsent normal draft or credentials. Retain the file
until later readers no longer need it. See docs/conversation-history.md.
.SS "History and reload"
Use /resume to select saved conversation text for the current workspace and
continue appending to it. The versioned JSONL schema is ssmcp.conversation.v1,
with system/user/assistant/tool roles and string content. History is redacted.
Restoring text never resends old requirements, reruns tools, resumes an external
harness session or restores saved credentials. See docs/conversation-history.md
for storage paths, capacity limits and schema details.
.PP
/reload and /restart restart the client connection and restore its controlled
draft/queue handoff. Existing connection-owned tasks end according to the
session lifecycle. Uncertain delivery is not replayed automatically.
F5 and the native UI action named reload only reload appearance.
.SS "Local execution tasks"
/tasks displays the actual local execution jobs. Use /tasks cancel JOB_ID only
when explicitly cancelling that exact job. Listing tasks does not cancel them.
.SS "Actual harness tools"
The local /pi, /cc and /codex commands display the current installed registry.
Adding tools OFFSET pages the directory; adding TOOL alone inspects that
tool's original descriptor and input schema. Only TOOL followed by an explicit
JSON object invokes it asynchronously, retaining native authorization rules.
The cc alias uses Claude's canonical namespace. Missing tools report an error;
discovery neither installs software nor starts a model turn.
.PP
Pi RPC get_commands lists commands, skills or templates; it is not a catalog
of internal LSP, model or plugin tools. The client does not invent such
interfaces. See docs/client-input.md for reference syntax and examples.
.SS "Alternate frontends"
Alternate frontends use ssmcp.ui.get/set/action/filter/queue, local
ssmcp.ui.message/ask/questions, read-only ssmcp.ui.history, and read-only
ssmcp.ui.harness catalog inspection. UI get supports JSON Pointer projections
and array/string pages, bounded to 8 KiB per projected response. Pure UI polling
does not implicitly claim next-caller requirements.

.SH PLATFORMS
The client supports Linux, macOS and Windows. Linux releases contain ssmcp and
ssmcpd; macOS and Windows releases contain the client. Windows uses ConPTY and
requires OpenSSH Client. Rsync endpoints are currently Linux-only.
Release archives cover x86_64 and ARM64 and include SHA-256 checksums.
.SH MIGRATION
Version 0.5 removes -t, --type and --backend; passing them reports migration
instructions. Existing SSH configuration, public-key authentication, OAuth,
name, title and allow settings remain applicable. Use --list-tools to inspect
the current registry. Discovery must not install software or start model
requests; providers without a verified safe path remain unavailable.
Explicit native calls may enter model workflows and require task authorization.
